From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 01:28:18 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 03/17] Boot from an encrypted (LUKS2) system volume

The boot loader (src/system/boot/loader/luks.cpp, enabled for the EFI
loaders of all architectures, BOOT_SUPPORT_LUKS):
- a partition with neither a partition table nor a known file system but
  a LUKS2 header is kept as a locked volume, with its own descriptor of the
  device (the loader recreates Partition objects when it scans again; the
  same volume seen twice is one);
- with no other boot volume, it asks for the passphrase right away (no
  echo, Backspace, Esc for the boot menu, asks again after a wrong one);
  the boot menu's volume list has "Unlock encrypted volume ..." items;
- an EncryptedDisk node decrypts the volume and is scanned like a disk, so
  the BFS volume inside becomes the boot volume;
- the volume's UUID and key go into the boot volume message, as its last
  field (a later one could make the message grow and leave a copy of the
  key in the freed buffer); right after the message is copied to the kernel
  args, the key, the ciphers and the loader's copy are wiped.

The kernel (vfs_boot.cpp) takes the keys out of the boot volume message and
wipes them there before anything reads it, opens the partition with that
UUID through luks_disk's boot module after the first device scan, scans
again, and finds the boot partition on the decrypted device by the usual
disk identifier; then its copy of the keys is wiped. No second prompt.
Using the boot volume message rather than a kernel_args field keeps older
kernels bootable from this loader and older loaders working with this
kernel.

The EFI loader switches off the firmware's 5 minute watchdog, which reset
the machine when someone took longer at the boot menu or the passphrase
prompt.

Tested in QEMU (x86_64, UEFI/OVMF, NVMe), src/tests/system/luks2/qemu/
run-boot-test.sh, 13 checks: the passphrase typed through OVMF's serial
console; with AES-NI and with the portable AES; a wrong passphrase first;
through the boot menu; a 512 MiB, 4-lane argon2id keyslot added by
cryptsetup; no copy of the whole volume key (nor the passphrase) left in
the guest's RAM after boot; what Haiku wrote read back on the build host.
arm64_compile_check.sh compiles all LUKS2 sources for arm64.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
